{"type":"lesson.started","request_id":"fb68d21c-70fb-4f0c-8fcd-cee90666637b","title":"Pythagorean theorem with squares on each side"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"fb68d21c-70fb-4f0c-8fcd-cee90666637b","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Start with a right triangle. The two legs meet at a right angle; the opposite side is the hypotenuse.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"squares","region":"left","frame":{"x":[-4.5,7.5],"y":[-3.5,7.5],"axes":"none"},"marks":[{"kind":"point","id":"p","at":[0,0]},{"kind":"point","id":"q","at":[3,0]},{"kind":"point","id":"r","at":[0,4]},{"kind":"polyline","points":[{"at":"p"},{"at":"q"},{"at":"r"}],"closed":true},{"kind":"polyline","points":[[0.35,0],[0.35,0.35],[0,0.35]]},{"kind":"text","at":[1.5,0.35],"text":"a = 3"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.55,2],"text":"b = 4"},{"kind":"text","at":[1.9,2.3],"text":"c"}]},{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"C1","content":"Pythagorean theorem"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"fb68d21c-70fb-4f0c-8fcd-cee90666637b","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Build a square outward on each leg. Their areas are three squared and four squared: nine and sixteen.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"legs","on":"squares","marks":[{"kind":"polyline","points":[{"at":"p"},[0,-3],[3,-3],{"at":"q"}],"closed":true,"fill":true},{"kind":"polyline","points":[{"at":"p"},{"at":"r"},[-4,4],[-4,0]],"closed":true,"fill":true},{"kind":"text","at":[1.5,-1.5],"text":"9"},{"kind":"text","at":[-2,2],"text":"16"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"legareas","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"a^2+b^2=3^2+4^2=9+16"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"fb68d21c-70fb-4f0c-8fcd-cee90666637b","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Now build a square on the hypotenuse. The theorem says its area equals the combined areas of the two smaller squares.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"hyp","on":"squares","marks":[{"kind":"polyline","points":[{"at":"q"},[7,3],[4,7],{"at":"r"}],"closed":true,"fill":true},{"kind":"text","at":[3.5,3.5],"text":"25"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"theorem","anchor":{"el":"legareas","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"\\boxed{a^2+b^2=c^2}"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"If the square on the hypotenuse has area 25, what is the hypotenuse's length?","expected_gist":"Its length is the square root of 25, which is 5."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"fb68d21c-70fb-4f0c-8fcd-cee90666637b","step":{"id":"s4","script":"Area twenty-five means side length five. We add square areas, not side lengths. This relationship holds for every right triangle.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"length","on":"squares","marks":[{"kind":"measure","from":[3,0],"to":[7,3],"label":"c = 5"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"answer","anchor":{"el":"theorem","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"c=\\sqrt{9+16}=\\sqrt{25}=5"},{"op":"text","id":"condition","anchor":{"el":"answer","side":"below","gap":0.1},"content":"Right triangles only"}],"checkpoint":null}}
